|~ The verbs of tagged values [D62]. Each resides on `::qlang/tagged`,
   the kind of every value under a tag, which the walk of a tagged value
   reaches after its own tag [D72], [D78]. ~|

:payload
  |~~ The value the tag holds, the reverse of `tag`: `value | tag ::Foo
      | payload | eq value` holds of every value. A set's payload is its
      vector.

      ~(::Box {} | ::Box[42] | payload | eq [42])
      ~(::Pair {} | ::Pair[1 2] | payload | eq [1 2])
      ~(::Note {} | ::Note"hello" | payload | eq "hello")
      ~(#[2 1] | payload | eq [1 2])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/payload})

::PayloadSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError
  |~~ `payload` requires a TaggedInstance subject (a value built
      through a `::Tag<payload>` constructor invocation).
      ~(
        42 | payload
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::PayloadSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:within
  |~~ An edit under one tag: `tagged | within ~(…)` runs the quote
      against the payload as `apply` runs it, a fork whose declarations
      stay inside, and the answer returns the subject's kind, minted
      back under its tag, whose constructor runs once, at the rewrap
      [D41], [D67]. The steps between may break the tag's invariant,
      since an invariant holds of the result: a set's constructor
      normalizes the vector the edit answers, where a record's refuses
      it. An error the edit answers passes as it is, and a deeper stack
      of tags is reached by nesting `within`.

      ~(#[3 1] | within ~([/0 /1 /0]) | eq #[1 3])
      ~(::Box {} | ::Box[1 2] | within ~(reverse) | eq ::Box[2 1])
      ~(::Box {} | ::Box#[3 1] | within ~(within ~([/1 /0 2])) | eq ::Box#[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:code ::quote | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/within})

::WithinSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError
  |~~ `within` requires a TaggedInstance subject, a value that has a
      tag to edit under.
      ~(
        [1 2] | within ~(reverse)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::WithinSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|

::WithinCodeNotQuoteError
  |~~ `within` requires its captured arg to answer a Quote — the
      edit it runs against the payload.
      ~(
        #[3 1] | within 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::WithinCodeNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
